        <div id="identify-surface" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. First, Identify Your Driveway Surface
          </h2>

          <p>
            This sounds obvious, but it trips up a surprising number of people, especially new homeowners and anyone dealing with an older driveway that has been patched, coated or resurfaced over the years. Asphalt and concrete can both be gray, both crack, and both get dirty. The material underneath determines everything about the sealer you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Asphalt</strong> (also called blacktop) is stones bound in a black, petroleum-based binder. It is slightly flexible, softens on a hot summer day, and you can often press a fingernail or key into the surface on a warm afternoon. When new, it is black. As it ages, the binder oxidizes and the surface turns gray, exposing individual stones. It typically has no control joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Concrete</strong> is cement, sand and stone that cure into a rigid slab. It is hard regardless of temperature, starts out light gray or white, and has straight cut or tooled lines every eight to twelve feet called control joints. Cracks in concrete tend to be sharper and straighter. Stamped, colored or exposed-aggregate driveways are all concrete.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHEN IN DOUBT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Look at the edge and the joints.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Asphalt edges crumble and slope into the lawn. Concrete edges are formed square, and concrete almost always has evenly spaced straight joints. If you see both, you may have an asphalt overlay on old concrete, which should be treated as asphalt.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Pavers are a third category, made of individual concrete or clay units set on sand. They take concrete-family sealers, but joint sand changes the process. If you are dealing with interlocking pavers, our guide to the <a href="/best-paver-sealer/">best paver sealers</a> covers products and joint stabilization in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-different" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why Asphalt and Concrete Need Different Sealers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Asphalt and concrete deteriorate through different mechanisms, which is why the sealers that protect them work in opposite ways. Understanding the failure mode makes the product choice obvious.
          </p>

          <p>
            Asphalt fails mainly from the top down through oxidation. Sunlight and air harden the binder, the surface becomes brittle and porous, fine material washes out, and water gets in. An asphalt sealer replaces that lost binder with a fresh sacrificial layer that sits on the surface, restores the black color, and takes the UV and wear for a few years so the pavement does not have to.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete does not oxidize in the same way. Its main enemy is water, especially water carrying dissolved deicing salt. Water soaks into the pores, freezes, expands and pops off thin flakes of the surface, a process called scaling. Salt makes it worse by increasing the number of freeze-thaw cycles and, in some forms, attacking the cement paste chemically. A concrete sealer protects mainly by keeping water and salt out of the pores.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mixing them up fails in predictable ways. Asphalt sealer on concrete has nothing similar to bond with, peels in sheets, tracks black into the house and makes a light slab look like a spill. A penetrating concrete sealer on asphalt cannot react with the binder and simply evaporates. There is no universal driveway sealer that does both jobs well, whatever a label might imply.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="concrete-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Concrete Sealer Types: Penetrating vs Film-Forming
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete sealers split into two broad groups. Penetrating sealers soak in and work inside the pores. Film-forming sealers leave a coating on top. Each has a clear use case on a driveway.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Look</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Reseal</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best Use</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Silane / siloxane (penetrating)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Invisible, natural</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often 5+ years, per label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plain concrete driveways in salt and freeze climates</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lithium or sodium silicate (densifier)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Invisible</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rarely needed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hardening weak or dusting surfaces, often paired with a repellent</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Acrylic (film-forming)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Satin to wet-look gloss</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stamped, colored and exposed-aggregate concrete</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Epoxy / polyurethane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High gloss</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Garage floors, not exterior driveways</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            <strong>Penetrating silane and siloxane sealers</strong> are the default for an ordinary concrete driveway. They do not change appearance, do not wear off under tires, are not slippery, and are cited by many state transportation departments and concrete industry groups as an effective defense against chloride intrusion. Our dedicated guide to <a href="/best-penetrating-concrete-sealer/">penetrating concrete sealers</a> compares concentrations and carriers.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Acrylic sealers</strong> are the choice when appearance matters. They darken and enrich color, give a satin or wet-look sheen, and resist staining while intact. They also wear, especially where tires turn, and need recoating every few years. Solvent-based acrylics generally penetrate and bond better than water-based ones but carry more fumes and stricter VOC rules.
          </p>

          <p>
            Epoxy and polyurethane coatings are excellent indoors but a poor match for exterior driveways. Most epoxies yellow under UV, and both can trap moisture coming up from the ground and delaminate. Keep them in the garage, where our <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">garage floor epoxy guide</a> covers the right products. For a broader concrete-only comparison, see our <a href="/best-concrete-driveway-sealer-reviews/">concrete driveway sealer reviews</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="asphalt-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Asphalt Sealer Options in Brief
          </h2>

          <p>
            The asphalt side of the market is simpler, because nearly every retail product is a variation on asphalt emulsion: refined asphalt suspended in water with clay, plus varying amounts of filler, sand and polymer. The differences are mostly in thickness and additives.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thin, unfilled emulsions are maintenance coats for smooth driveways sealed recently. Filled emulsions add mineral filler to cover texture and bridge hairline cracks, which is what most aging driveways need. Polymer-modified emulsions add latex or rubber for flexibility and scuff resistance and sit at the top of most brand lines.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coal tar sealers are still sold in some areas and resist fuel and oil better than asphalt-based ones, but they contain high levels of polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, carry stronger odor and skin-irritation risks, and are restricted in Washington, Minnesota and many municipalities. Oil-based cutback products are a small niche with strong solvent fumes. For a home driveway, a good asphalt emulsion is the right family.
          </p>

          <p>
            We go much deeper on asphalt products in our ranked list of the <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">best asphalt driveway sealers</a>, and if you want to understand what is actually in the pail and how retail products compare with what contractors spray, our guide to <a href="/best-asphalt-sealer/">asphalt sealer chemistry</a> explains the formulations.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="climate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Climate, Deicers and What Actually Damages Driveways
          </h2>

          <p>
            Where you live should shape the decision as much as the surface. A concrete driveway in Arizona and one in Minnesota face very different threats, and the same is true of asphalt in Florida sun versus a New England winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cold climates with road salt:</strong> For concrete, a penetrating silane-siloxane sealer is close to essential, and the first two winters matter most because young concrete is most vulnerable. Avoid deicers on new concrete during its first winter if you can, and avoid magnesium chloride and ammonium-based products on any concrete, since they are generally considered harsher on cement paste than plain rock salt or calcium chloride. Our guide to <a href="/best-ice-melt-for-concrete/">ice melt that is safer for concrete</a> sorts products by risk.
          </p>

          <p>
            For asphalt in cold climates, cracks are the weak point. Water gets in, freezes and widens them every winter, so crack filling before sealing matters more than which pail you choose. Snowplows and shovels also scrape sealer off, so expect high-traffic strips to wear faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hot, sunny climates:</strong> UV is the dominant force on asphalt, and sealing on a regular schedule pays off more here than anywhere. For concrete, salt is less of a concern, so the case for sealing rests more on stain resistance and appearance. An acrylic sealer can make sense on decorative concrete, but plain gray concrete in a warm, dry climate can often go without sealer at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="special-surfaces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Pavers, Stamped Concrete and Exposed Aggregate
          </h2>

          <p>
            Decorative driveways are where film-forming sealers earn their place. Stamped concrete usually has color hardener and release agents on the surface that fade and wash out without a sealer, and the original installer almost always applied an acrylic. Recoating with a compatible acrylic is the normal maintenance. Switching to a penetrating sealer will not restore the color or sheen, and switching between incompatible acrylics can cause a cloudy, peeling finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Exposed-aggregate concrete looks best with a wet-look acrylic that brings out the stone colors, and it also benefits from the acrylic binding loose fines at the surface. Keep coats thin. Thick acrylic on exposed aggregate tends to puddle in the low spots and turn white if moisture gets trapped beneath it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pavers need the joint sand addressed first. If you are re-sanding with polymeric sand, it has to cure before sealing, and some sealers are designed to stabilize ordinary joint sand themselves. Our guides to the <a href="/best-polymeric-sand/">best polymeric sand</a> and <a href="/best-stamped-concrete-sealer/">stamped concrete sealers</a> cover the sequencing and product compatibility.
          </p>

          <p>
            The one firm rule for all decorative concrete: test any new sealer on a small, out-of-the-way patch first, and wait a day before judging. Compatibility problems usually show up as whitening, bubbling or lifting within hours, and finding that out on a square foot is far better than finding it out on the whole driveway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Application Differences by Surface
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both surfaces need to be clean and dry before sealing, but the process diverges from there. Concrete sealers are usually thin liquids applied with a pump sprayer or roller. Asphalt sealers are thick, black and applied with a squeegee or brush.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Concrete:</strong> pressure wash, let the slab dry for at least a day, and protect plants and anything you do not want sealed. For a penetrating sealer, spray until the surface is evenly wet without puddles, back-roll or broom any puddles, and apply a second coat wet-on-wet if the label calls for it. For an acrylic, use thin coats, roll out any puddles quickly, and let each coat dry fully. New concrete generally needs about 28 days to cure before sealing, and our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-seal-a-concrete-driveway/">sealing a concrete driveway</a> walks through each stage.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Asphalt:</strong> trim edges, clean, treat oil spots with a primer, fill cracks and let the filler cure. Stir the sealer thoroughly, cut in the edges with a brush, then pour and spread with a squeegee in overlapping passes. Two thin coats are better than one heavy one. Our full guide on <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">how to seal an asphalt driveway</a> includes tools and timing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weather windows are similar for both: most products want temperatures of roughly 50 degrees Fahrenheit and above through the cure, no rain for at least a day, and no direct blazing sun on a scorching surface. Penetrating concrete sealers can be more forgiving of cooler temperatures than asphalt emulsions, but follow each label.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="schedule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Often to Reseal Each Surface
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Reseal Intervals</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2-3 yr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Asphalt, water-based emulsion</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5+ yr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Concrete, silane-siloxane per many labels</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2-3 yr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Concrete, acrylic film</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">28 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical wait after a new concrete pour</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The water test works for both surfaces. Sprinkle water on a dry day. If it beads up or runs off, the sealer is still working. If it darkens the surface and soaks in, it is time. On concrete with a penetrating sealer, the beading effect may fade gradually from the most-trafficked areas first, so test the tire tracks rather than the edges.
          </p>

          <p>
            Over-sealing asphalt is a real problem. Each coat adds thickness, and a heavy buildup tends to crack and flake, taking good layers with it. Sealing on a fixed annual schedule is a common way to cause this. Our guide on <a href="/how-often-should-you-seal-your-driveway/">how often to seal a driveway</a> explains how to read the surface rather than the calendar.
          </p>

          <p>
            Acrylic sealers on concrete fail visibly: the gloss fades unevenly, tire paths go dull and color looks washed out. Recoat when that happens, using the same type of acrylic. Penetrating sealers fail invisibly, which is why the water test matters more for them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Cost by Surface
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface and Sealer</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">DIY Material Cost per Sq Ft</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Asphalt, budget emulsion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $0.10-$0.20</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two coats, porous surfaces cost more</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Asphalt, premium filled emulsion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $0.15-$0.30</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Better film, often fewer touch-ups</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete, silane-siloxane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $0.15-$0.40</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Long interval lowers yearly cost</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete, solvent acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $0.15-$0.35</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Add anti-slip additive for slopes</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those ranges are rough figures at the time of writing and vary by region and product. The more useful comparison is cost per year of protection. A penetrating concrete sealer that lasts five years or more per its label can be cheaper per year than an acrylic that needs recoating every two to three years, even if the initial gallon price is higher.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tools add a little. A pump sprayer and roller for concrete run modestly, and a squeegee, brush, drill mixing paddle and crack filler for asphalt add a bit more. Professional sealing runs higher per square foot but saves a weekend, and on very large asphalt driveways contractors' bulk pricing can match DIY. Our <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-seal-a-driveway/">driveway sealing cost guide</a> has detailed figures for hired jobs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where to spend: on concrete, buy a quality penetrating sealer and skip expensive coatings unless the driveway is decorative. On asphalt, spend on crack filling and prep before you spend on a premium pail. A mid-priced sealer on a properly prepared surface outlasts a flagship sealer on a dirty, cracked one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using asphalt sealer on concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                This is the most expensive mistake on the list. Asphalt sealer does not bond to concrete, peels in sheets within a season, tracks black onto floors and turns a light slab dark and blotchy. Removing it takes chemical strippers and pressure washing. If your driveway has straight joints and is hard on a hot day, it is concrete.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing concrete too soon after pouring</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh concrete releases moisture for weeks. Sealing it early can trap that moisture, causing a cloudy film or poor bonding with acrylics. The common guidance is to wait about 28 days unless the sealer label specifically allows earlier application. Ask your concrete contractor what curing compound they used, because it can affect sealer bonding.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying acrylic on too thick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thick acrylic coats trap solvent and moisture, which shows up as white haze, bubbles and peeling. They also make the surface more slippery. Two thin coats look better and bond better than one heavy one. On sloped driveways, add an anti-slip grit to the final coat to keep traction in rain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping crack repair on asphalt</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer does not fill open cracks. It sinks in, leaves a channel for water and shows the crack again within weeks. Cracks wider than about an eighth of an inch need a dedicated filler and time to cure before sealing. In cold climates, unfilled cracks widen every winter and undermine the whole driveway.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing gloss when you need protection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A wet-look finish is attractive, but on a plain concrete driveway in a salt-belt climate it is the wrong priority. Acrylics wear under tires and need frequent recoating, while the main threat, salt-driven scaling, is better handled by a penetrating sealer. Choose the look you want only after the protection question is settled.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can one sealer be used on both asphalt and concrete driveways?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Asphalt sealers are black emulsions that coat the surface, and concrete sealers are clear products that either penetrate the pores or form a clear film. Neither works on the other material. Asphalt sealer peels off concrete and stains it, and concrete penetrating sealers have nothing to react with in asphalt. Buy the product made for your surface.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best sealer for a plain concrete driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A water-based or solvent-based silane-siloxane penetrating sealer, such as Foundation Armor SX5000 or Ghostshield Siloxa-Tek 8500. These soak in, leave no visible film, are not slippery, and reduce water and salt absorption, which is the main cause of winter surface damage. Choose an acrylic only if you want a darker, glossier look and accept more frequent recoating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a wet-look sealer bad for a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not bad, but it comes with tradeoffs. Wet-look acrylics make concrete slippery when wet, wear under tires and usually need recoating every two to three years. They are the right choice for stamped, colored or exposed-aggregate driveways where appearance is the point. On sloped driveways, mix an anti-slip additive into the final coat to keep traction in rain and snow.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I seal a brand new driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but not immediately. New concrete typically needs about 28 days to cure before most sealers. New asphalt needs considerably longer, usually six months to a year, so its oils can cure out and the surface can harden. Sealing either one too early leads to poor bonding. Ask your installer for their specific guidance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know when my driveway needs sealing again?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use the water test on a dry day. If water beads or runs off, the existing protection is still working. If it darkens the surface and soaks in within a minute or two, it is time to reseal. On asphalt, graying and exposed stones are also clear signs. On acrylic-sealed concrete, dull, uneven gloss in tire paths means a recoat is due.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealing a concrete driveway prevent cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not structural cracks. Those come from settlement, tree roots, heavy loads or poor base preparation. What sealing does prevent is surface scaling and spalling from water and salt, along with staining. It also slows the widening of small cracks by keeping water out. For cracks already present, fill them with a concrete crack filler before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are coal tar driveway sealers still legal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on where you live. Washington and Minnesota restrict coal tar sealers statewide, and many cities and counties elsewhere ban their sale or use. Even where they remain legal, they carry stronger odor, skin and eye irritation risk during application, and environmental concerns tied to runoff. For a home driveway, an asphalt emulsion is the practical choice.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
